  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Last day of above average precipitation chances as a \nstalled upper trough that had brought multiple days of heavy rain \nand thunderstorm activity to the area finally shifts north and gets \nabsorbed by an incoming upper level trough moving through the Great \nLakes region. At the same time, mid level shortwave energy shifts to \nthe north and west as well. On top of this, for the rest of the \nafternoon, surface boundary to the north and west of the area \ncontinues to gradually dissipate while S'rly flow keeps PWATs around \nthe 2-2.5\" range today. Any leftover upper level forcing combined \nwith the daily seabreeze should be enough to kick start additional \nrounds of isolated showers and weak thunderstorms this \nafternoon and evening with heavy to torrential rainfall \npossible. While storm motions should be progressive, storms will\nbe capable of dropping a quick 1-2\", resulting in minor \nflooding concerns. After we lose daytime heating, rain chances \ngo down drastically. Highs today have gotten into the mid 80s \nwith lows tonight forecast to be in the 70s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A more zonal pattern aloft sets up as upper troughs \nand mid level shortwaves generally remain to the north of the \nCarolinas. This will result in a return to more avg to below avg \nrain chances across ENC from Thur on into next week as main forcing \nwill be from the daily seabreeze. While chances will certainly be \nlower ample instability will build across the region given the \npartly cloudy skies and moist environment so wouldn't be shocked at a \ndaily 20-30% chance of shower or thunderstorm activity from Thurs \nonwards along he inland moving seabreeze each afternoon. Given the \nlower precip chances the main story from Thurs onwards will be the \nreturn of the heat with highs getting into the upper 80s to low 90s \neach day into next week. COmbined with dewpoints generally in the \n70s each day will bring heat indices between 95-105F each day. While \nbelow heat headline criteria this will make it feel uncomfortably \nhot over the next few days. Not much relief overnight as lows get \ninto the 70s each night.",
